writing:{
 title:'Task 1 – Process diagram (band 6.5)',
 prompt:'The diagram below shows how biogas is produced from food waste and used on a farm. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features. Write at least 150 words.',
 chart:{type:'process',title:'Producing biogas from food waste',per:4,steps:['Food waste|collected','Plastic & metal|removed','Waste shredded|+ mixed with water','Fed into|sealed digester','Broken down|by bacteria (3 weeks)','Biogas → storage|→ cooking & electricity','Digestate|→ fertiliser for crops','Crops grown|→ food (cycle restarts)']},
 min:150,max:200,
 wex:[
  ['Overview band 6.5:',['Overall, food waste is collected.','Overall, the process consists of eight stages, beginning with the collection of food waste and producing two useful outputs, biogas and fertiliser; it is cyclical, as the fertiliser helps grow new crops.','Overall, biogas is good for the environment.'],1,'Số bước + đầu – cuối + 2 đầu ra + tính tuần hoàn.'],
  ['Câu bị động đúng:',['The waste is shred into small pieces.','The waste is shredded into small pieces.','The waste shredded into small pieces.'],1,'is + V3 (shredded).'],
  ['Mệnh đề quan hệ rút gọn đúng:',['The biogas, which collected in a tank, is used for cooking.','The biogas, collected in a storage tank, is used for cooking and electricity.','The biogas collecting in a tank is used.'],1,'V3 rút gọn = which is collected.'],
  ['Từ nối thứ tự đa dạng nhất:',['Then… then… then…','Initially… Following this,… Once…, … At the final stage,…','First… second… third…'],1,'Đa dạng từ nối thứ tự.'],
  ['Không nên làm gì trong Task 1 Process?',['đưa ý kiến biogas tốt hay xấu','dùng bị động','chia 2 đoạn thân bài'],0,'Task 1 chỉ mô tả.'],
  ['Chia đoạn hợp lý:',['Đoạn 1: chuẩn bị rác & đưa vào bể; Đoạn 2: phân hủy, 2 sản phẩm và chu trình lặp lại','Mỗi bước một đoạn','Một đoạn duy nhất'],0,'2 giai đoạn rõ ràng.']],
 outline:['Mở bài: The diagram illustrates how food waste is converted into biogas and fertiliser on a farm.','Overview: 8 giai đoạn; từ thu gom rác đến 2 đầu ra (khí sinh học + phân bón); quy trình tuần hoàn.','Thân bài 1: thu gom → loại nhựa, kim loại → nghiền + trộn nước → đưa vào bể kín.','Thân bài 2: vi khuẩn phân hủy 3 tuần → biogas vào bể chứa → nấu ăn + điện; digestate → phân bón → cây trồng → thực phẩm → rác (lặp lại).'],
 model:'The diagram illustrates how food waste is converted into biogas and fertiliser on a farm.<br><br><i>Overall</i>, the process consists of eight stages, beginning with the collection of food waste and resulting in two useful products: biogas for energy and fertiliser for crops. The process is <b>cyclical</b>, as the crops eventually become food and, later, waste once again.<br><br><i>Initially</i>, food waste <b>is collected</b>, and any plastic and metal <b>are removed</b>. <i>Following this</i>, the remaining organic material <b>is shredded</b> and mixed with water, <i>before</i> the mixture <b>is fed into</b> a sealed digester.<br><br>Inside the digester, the waste <b>is broken down</b> by bacteria over a period of approximately three weeks. <i>This produces</i> biogas, which <b>is transferred</b> to a storage tank and then used for cooking and generating electricity. <i>At the same time</i>, the material left over, known as digestate, is used as fertiliser for crops. <i>At the final stage</i>, these crops are grown and harvested as food, which means the cycle can begin again.',
 note:'Khoảng 170 từ, band 6.5. Overview nêu số bước, điểm đầu, hai đầu ra và tính tuần hoàn. Bị động đa dạng: is collected, are removed, is shredded, is fed into, is broken down, is transferred.',
 checklist:['Overview: số bước + đầu – cuối + tuần hoàn','Ít nhất 6 động từ bị động khác nhau','Có 1 mệnh đề quan hệ (which is…) hoặc rút gọn','Từ nối thứ tự đa dạng','Không đưa ý kiến cá nhân','≥ 150 từ trong 20 phút']
},
speaking:{
 task:'Ghi âm Part 2 (2 phút) và cả 3 câu Part 3, gửi cô.',
 items:[
 ['Do you try to save energy at home?',"Yes, my family is quite careful. We switch off lights and fans when we leave a room, and my parents only turn on the air conditioner at night when it's really hot."],
 ['Is recycling common where you live?',"It's becoming more common. People have always sold paper and cans to collectors, but separating food waste is still quite new in my area."],
 ['Do you think young people care about the environment?',"I think many do, especially about plastic pollution and climate change. But caring and changing habits are different things, and I include myself in that."],
 ['Would you like to work in the energy industry?',"Maybe in renewable energy. Vietnam has huge potential for solar and wind power, so I think there will be a lot of interesting jobs in that field."]],
 part2:{cue:'Describe something you do to help protect the environment.',bullets:['what you do','when you started doing it','how difficult or easy it is','and explain why you think it is important.'],
  notes:'stop using single-use plastic: own bottle, cloth bag, metal straw · started grade 10 after class project on ocean plastic · easy at home, harder with friends/takeaway · plastic takes centuries to decompose, Vietnam coastline',
  model:"Something I do to protect the environment is avoiding single-use plastic as much as possible. I carry my own water bottle to school, I keep a cloth bag in my backpack for shopping, and I use a metal straw when I buy drinks. I started doing this in grade ten, after our class did a project on plastic pollution in the ocean. We watched a documentary showing beaches covered in plastic bottles and bags, and one of them was actually in central Vietnam, which really shocked me. To be honest, some parts are easy and some are harder. At home and at school it's simple, because I always have my bottle with me. It's more difficult when I go out with friends and we order takeaway, because almost everything comes in plastic boxes. Sometimes I forget my bag, too, and end up taking a plastic one. I think it's important for two reasons. First, plastic takes hundreds of years to decompose, so every bag I avoid is one less piece of rubbish that might end up in the sea. Second, I think small habits can spread. Since I started, two of my friends have bought their own bottles as well, and my mum now takes a cloth bag to the market."},
 part3:[
 ['Should governments or individuals be responsible for protecting the environment?',"I'd argue that both have a role, but governments have far more power. Individuals can change their habits, but only governments can invest in renewable energy, set rules for factories and organise proper recycling systems. Without those systems, individual efforts have a limited impact."],
 ['Why don’t more people recycle?',"In many places, it's simply not convenient. If there aren't separate bins or regular collections, people won't make the effort. Some people also doubt that their recycling actually gets recycled, which reduces their motivation."],
 ['Will renewable energy completely replace fossil fuels?',"Eventually, I think it will, but probably not for several decades. Solar and wind power are getting cheaper, but countries still need reliable sources when the sun isn't shining. Technologies like batteries and biogas could help fill those gaps."]]
},
